{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792832410,"nonce":"cB11qs0Lzd7TTkrGcmcUiP1j5A8=","title":"sched: a simulation clock the world's timers follow","message":"ChimpMonk (a game engine on Caribou; its git-bug 5622c4b) steps its simulation at a fixed rate and promises deterministic replay. The core scheduler reads `Instant::now()` directly for its timer heap, park deadlines and sleeps (sched/world.rs, wait.rs), so every wait follows wall time.\n\nWanted: a clock the host owns, set on the world.\n- `now()`, and `advance_to(t)`, which the host calls once per fixed step.\n- Timers keyed by that clock.\n- Equal deadlines wake in the order they were registered, so a replay gives the same wake order.\n\nEvery timed wait in the process follows the clock: Haxe's `Sys.sleep` and timers through Ash's seam (`sleep_ns`, `park`), Wren's, Zyntax's `sleep`, and the Lua and Python timers that reach the core.\n\nThe world's own pacing (watch_sources, the reactor's poll) stays on wall time.\n\nChimpMonk runs on Caribou as its runtime, so this is the engine's whole timing story, not only scripts'.","files":null}]}